  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Emergency Humanitarian Response to the Mixed Flows Crossing the Darien Jungle</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Despite efforts by host countries to regularize and integrate refugees and migrants from Venezuela, more than four million people still face difficulties accessing food, shelter, health care, education and formal employment in Latin America and the Caribbean. Moreover, amidst a global and regional cost-of-living crisis, Venezuelans are at heightened risk of being victims of abuses such as trafficking in persons, forced recruitment and gender-based violence. 
 
Through this project, the International Organization for Migration (IOM) is providing lifesaving humanitarian assistance to vulnerable migrants in transit or stranded at the Temporary Migrant Reception Stations in the Darien or in remote reception communities in the Comarca Embera Wounaan. IOM is providing essential services and assistance to improve their living conditions and dignity, reduce migrants&#x2019; suffering and avoid loss of life. This multi-sectoral action includes Camp Coordination and Camp Management (CCCM) activities at the Temporary Migrant Reception Stations, such as regular site visits to assess and refer urgent populations&#x2019; needs to relevant services and facilitate CCCM refresher trainings for humanitarian staff and government partners; providing access to food, health, essential non-food items, and protection assistance; and improving emergency shelter conditions.</narrative>
  </description>
